Let $v(r)$ be the velocity of a star at a distance $r$ from the center of the Galaxy. The gravitational force on a star of mass $m$ at a distance $r$ from the center of the Galaxy is given by
\begin{align*} \label{eq:1} F &= \frac{G m M(r)}{r^2} \end{align*}
where $M(r)$ is the mass enclosed within a sphere of radius $r$. Since the density of the dark matter is given by $\rho_{\text {dark }}(r)=\rho_{0}\left(\frac{r_{0}}{r}\right)^{2}$, the mass enclosed within a sphere of radius $r$ is
\begin{align*} M(r) &= \int_0^r 4 \pi r'^2 \rho_{\text {dark }}(r') dr' \\ &= \int_0^r 4 \pi r'^2 \rho_{0}\left(\frac{r_{0}}{r'}\right)^2 dr' \\ &= 4 \pi \rho_{0} \int_0^r r'^2 \left(\frac{r_{0}}{r'}\right)^2 dr' \\ &= 4 \pi \rho_{0} \int_0^r r'^2 \frac{r_{0}^2}{r'^2} dr' \\ &= 4 \pi \rho_{0} r_{0}^2 \int_0^r dr' \\ &= 4 \pi \rho_{0} r_{0}^2 r \end{align*}
Thus, the gravitational force on a star of mass $m$ at a distance $r$ from the center of the Galaxy is
\begin{align*} F &= \frac{G m M(r)}{r^2} \\ &= \frac{G m (4 \pi \rho_{0} r_{0}^2 r)}{r^2} \\ &= \frac{4 \pi G m \rho_{0} r_{0}^2}{r} \end{align*}
The centripetal force required to keep a star of mass $m$ moving in a circular orbit of radius $r$ is
\begin{align*} F &= \frac{m v^2}{r} \end{align*}
Equating the gravitational force to the centripetal force, we have
\begin{align*} \frac{4 \pi G m \rho_{0} r_{0}^2}{r} &= \frac{m v^2}{r} \\ v^2 &= \frac{4 \pi G \rho_{0} r_{0}^2}{r} \end{align*}
Thus, the velocity of a star at a distance $r$ from the center of the Galaxy is
\begin{align*} v(r) &= \sqrt{\frac{4 \pi G \rho_{0} r_{0}^2}{r}} \\ &= 2 \sqrt{\pi G \rho_{0} r_{0}^2} \frac{1}{\sqrt{r}} \end{align*}
This is the rotation curve of the Galaxy at large distances.

Final Answer: The final answer is $\boxed{2 \sqrt{\pi G \rho_{0} r_{0}^2} \frac{1}{\sqrt{r}}}$

